# tools/crypto_mldsa_identity_witness.rish — the POST-QUANTUM identity FRONT DOOR: the one composition that ties the whole ML-DSA ladder into a single
# answer, authored in pure Rye and proven by a doubled oracle against Zig's independent std.crypto sign.mldsa. It composes the GREEN keygen
# (crypto/mldsa_keygen.rye), deterministic signer (crypto/mldsa_sign.rye), and verifier (crypto/mldsa_verify.rye) so a caller reaches a quantum-resistant
# identity through one door: seed → keypair → signature → verdict (Season G).
#
# Season: crypto — the ML-DSA sibling of crypto/kumara_identity.rye. Where that renders a backup phrase to the Ed25519 identity every Grain module signs
# with today, this renders a 32-byte seed to the ML-DSA identity a keeper's name wants on the morning a large quantum computer breaks the old elliptic
# curves. The front door holds no key: every seed, message, and signature is a public test vector.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-161537_post-quantum-mlkem-mldsa-pivot.md ("its front door is the post-quantum successor to Kumara's Ed25519
# identity"). Rye-first spine: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md.
# Clean-room: ML-DSA is the public NIST FIPS 204 standard; the composition is our own Rye wiring over our own GREEN rungs, with Zig's std.crypto read only
# as the reference to diff against — never a copied line (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/mldsa_identity.rye proves itself four ways over every parameter set (ML-DSA-44/65/87) and four deterministic seeds with two messages each,
# no fabricated constant: (1) NO DRIFT — from_seed's keys equal keygen's own output byte-for-byte; (2) DETERMINISM — the same seed reaches an identical
# identity; (3) A REAL WORKING SIGNING KEY — it signs a message verify accepts, while a flipped message byte, a flipped signature byte, and a different
# identity's public key each refuse; (4) THE DOUBLED ORACLE — the identity's public key equals Zig's independent std.crypto newKeyFromSeed public key
# byte-for-byte, and Zig's own Signature.verify accepts the signature this front door produced. Purely local — no key held, no network, no funds, no device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_mldsa_identity_witness.rish

say "crypto-mldsa-identity: Language EN — the post-quantum identity FRONT DOOR: the one composition that ties the whole ML-DSA ladder (ring, encode, sample, keygen, sign, verify) into a single answer — seed then keypair then signature then verdict, reached through one door. from_seed composes ML-DSA.KeyGen_internal; sign and verify are thin wrappers over the GREEN signer and verifier, sized exactly per parameter set (ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65 the default, ML-DSA-87). It is the ML-DSA sibling of crypto/kumara_identity.rye — the identity a keeper's name wants on the morning the old elliptic curves fall."
say "crypto-mldsa-identity: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively as a whole identity confirmed twice. The front door authors no new cryptography; it wires already-proven rungs so a caller reaches a quantum-resistant identity through one call rather than six. Proven four ways: no drift from keygen, a deterministic identity, a real working signing key with tamper refused, and the doubled oracle — Zig's independent std.crypto growing the same seed to the same public key and accepting the same signature. Two strangers confirming one identity is the discipline the whole library rests on."
say "crypto-mldsa-identity: Lens TAME — contract asserts on every function (a real non-zero key written, the encoded widths matching the parameter set, the message within bound), bounded loops (four seeds, two messages, exactly the encoded byte lengths per copy), named errors for every disagreement (PublicDrift, SecretDrift, NotDeterministic, GenuineRejected, TamperMsgAccepted, TamperSigAccepted, SeedCollision, WrongKeyAccepted, OraclePublicMismatch, OracleRejectedGenuine). Correctness proven by a DOUBLED oracle: our composed front door versus Zig's independent std.crypto sign.mldsa, agreeing on the public key byte-for-byte and on the signature's acceptance at ML-DSA-44, ML-DSA-65, and ML-DSA-87 over four deterministic seeds and two messages each. The module's own functions call no std.crypto; only the selftest invokes Zig as the parity oracle. Every seed, message, and signature is a public test vector — no key held, no network, no funds, no device; growing a REAL post-quantum identity from the maintainer's own seed stays the custody gate, constant-time timing-safety the named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/mldsa_identity"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/mldsa_identity.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-mldsa-identity: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/mldsa_identity.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-mldsa-identity: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-mldsa-identity: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-mldsa-identity' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-mldsa-identity: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-mldsa-identity line"
